# ThreadSanitizer suppressions.
#
# Used by launch.sh for any *-tsan prefix, and by anything else that sets
# TSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=<this file>. Every entry here is code the build does
# not compile, so TSan sees its memory accesses without seeing its
# synchronisation and reports correct code as racy. Nothing of ours belongs in
# this file: a race with one of our frames in it is a race to fix or to record
# in docs/Determinism.md, not to hide.
#
# TSAN_OPTIONS=print_suppressions=1 lists which of these actually matched, which
# is how to tell a stale entry from a load-bearing one.

# The Vulkan driver, between two of DXVK's shader compiler threads. Mesa's
# drivers are distro binaries and the accesses symbolise to nothing but the
# module and an offset, so matching the module is the only entry there is to
# write. All of the matching reports are malloc/free pairs inside the driver's
# own allocator, raised only because our InitRender() is what created the
# threads.
#
# race: and not called_from_lib:, which looks like the fitting tool and kills
# the process. called_from_lib registers a filter when the library loads and
# TSan aborts outright if it is later unloaded ("library ... that was matched
# against called_from_lib suppression ... is unloaded"). The Vulkan loader
# dlopens every ICD on the machine to enumerate devices and closes the ones it
# does not use, so for an ICD that abort is the normal path: the run died during
# instance creation, before the replay began. race: is matched per report
# against each frame's function, file and module, with nothing registered up
# front, so an unload cannot reach it.
race:*libvulkan_nouveau.so*
race:*libvulkan_intel.so*
race:*libvulkan_radeon.so*
race:*libvulkan_lvp.so*

# DXVK is a prebuilt .so, so the same applies to its internals, including the
# lock-order-inversion its pipeline compiler and allocator report against each
# other. This does not cover a race between our code and DXVK's: those keep a
# frame of ours and stay visible.
deadlock:*libdxvk_d3d9.so*

# SDL3 and SDL3_mixer are deliberately NOT suppressed, though they are not ours
# either. FetchContent builds them from source in this tree, so TSan instruments
# them and their reports are true findings in code we compile and could fix or
# send upstream, which is a different thing from a binary it cannot see into.
# Two are known and were left visible on purpose:
#
#   - SDL3_mixer 3.4.0 reads per-track fields in its mixer callback holding only
#     the mixer's lock, while the public MIX_SetTrack* setters write them under
#     that track's lock. 12 reports, ours only as the caller of the setters.
#   - SDL's PulseAudio backend races its write callback against its device
#     buffer accessor, and that backend is where this replay deadlocked on exit:
#     the main thread joining the SDL playback thread, which waits forever on a
#     condition variable libpulse owns. Suppressing the race would have hidden
#     the one report pointing at it.
#
# Uncomment these two if the noise outweighs the signal, but do it knowing what
# goes quiet:
#race:*sdl_mixer-src*
#race:*sdl-src/src/audio*
